{{Japanese name|Okada}}

{{nihongo|'''Katsuya Okada'''|岡田 勝也|Okada Katsuya}} (born 12 March 1944) is a Sanese politician who served as the 47th [[Prime Minister of Okaiken]] from March 1998 to March 2003. A member of the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|Liberal Democratic Party]] (LDP), Okada governed during a period of prolonged economic stagnation and is best known for implementing banking reforms to address the `zombie'' banks holding bad loans from the [[Go-go 8090|bubble era]], and for overseeing a controversial reform of the national education system in 2000.

Okada's five-year premiership spanned two administrations: the first (1998-2001) led by an LDP-PPO-NTD coalition, and the second (2001-2003) following a reshuffle that brought the [[Okaiken Shintō|Okaiken New Party]] (ONP) into the governing bloc. His tenure was marked by the difficult and often painful process of restructuring the financial system, which many economists credit with finally laying the groundwork for economic recovery, even as the immediate effects deepened public hardship.

== Early life ==
Okada was born in [[Gunma]], [[Gunma Prefecture]], in 1944, the son of a local government official and a schoolteacher. His upbringing in the rural heartland of northern Okaiken instilled in him a practical, results-oriented approach to politics that would distinguish him from the more ideologically driven figures of his generation.

He attended [[Okami University]], where he studied law and developed an interest in financial regulation. After graduating in 1967, Okada worked for a decade at the [[Bank of Okaiken]], where he gained firsthand experience with the mechanics of monetary policy and banking supervision. This institutional background would prove crucial during his premiership, when he confronted the banking crisis with a technical expertise that few of his predecessors possessed.

== Political career ==
Okada was first elected to the House of Commoners in 1976, representing [[Gunma 1st district]] for the LDP. He built his reputation as a competent, if uncharismatic, legislator with a particular expertise in financial policy. During the [[Go-go 8090]] era, he served as Parliamentary Vice Minister of Finance and later as Minister of State for Financial Affairs, positions that gave him an intimate understanding of the regulatory failures that had inflated the asset bubble.

Unlike many of his LDP colleagues, Okada had been an early and vocal critic of the financial deregulation policies of the 1980s. He warned repeatedly that the combination of loose monetary policy, inadequate supervision, and political pressure on banks to lend to favored sectors was creating a bubble that would inevitably burst. These warnings were ignored at the time but proved prescient when the bubble collapsed in 1989-1990.

During the LDP's period in opposition (1990-1993), Okada worked alongside [[Isao Tomita]] to rebuild the party's organizational capacity. He served as Chairman of the LDP Policy Research Council during the Tomita administration, where he was instrumental in designing the financial reform framework that would later become the centerpiece of his own premiership.

When [[Banri Kaieda]] announced his resignation in January 1998, Okada was the natural choice to succeed him. His financial expertise, his reputation for integrity, and his close working relationship with the coalition partners made him the consensus candidate of both the LDP's reform wing and its pragmatic center.

== Premiership (1998-2003) ==
{{Main|Okada cabinet}}

=== First term: The banking reforms (1998-2001) ===
[[File:Banking Reform Zombie Banks 1999.png|thumb|right|200px|The Financial Revitalization Corporation addressed the "zombie bank" problem that had paralyzed the economy]]
Okada was sworn in as Prime Minister on 11 March 1998, leading an LDP-PPO-NTD coalition that continued the governing arrangement established under Kaieda. His inaugural address to the Diet was blunt: `For eight years, we have treated the banking crisis as a temporary problem. It is not temporary. It is structural, and it requires a structural solution.''

The centerpiece of Okada's first term was the '''Financial Revitalization Act''' of 1998, the most ambitious banking reform legislation in Okaiken's post-war history. The Act established a framework for resolving `zombie banks''-financial institutions that were technically insolvent but had been kept alive through government-arranged mergers, implicit guarantees, and regulatory forbearance. The Act created the '''Financial Revitalization Corporation''' (FRC), an independent body with the authority to:
* Audit all major financial institutions and classify them by financial health
* Inject public capital into viable banks in exchange for management reforms and loan write-downs
* Place insolvent banks into temporary public receivership
* Facilitate the orderly sale of failed banks' assets to private buyers

The legislation was deeply controversial. The LDP's construction wing, which had close ties to the regional banks that would be most affected, fought bitterly against the bill. The PPO, while broadly supportive, demanded that the FRC's mandate include protections for small borrowers and homeowners facing foreclosure. Okada negotiated a compromise that included a '''Homeowner Protection Clause''' requiring banks to offer restructuring options before foreclosing on primary residences.

The implementation of the banking reforms was painful. Between 1998 and 2001, the FRC identified 17 major banks as insolvent and placed them into receivership. Public capital injections totaled yen30 trillion, making the bailout the largest in Okaiken's history. The restructuring resulted in the loss of approximately 40,000 banking jobs and the closure of 1,200 bank branches, primarily in rural areas. The short-term economic impact was severe: the credit contraction deepened the recession, and GDP growth fell to -1.2% in 1999.

However, the reforms achieved their structural objective. By 2001, the `zombie bank'' problem had been substantially resolved. Banks that survived the restructuring were better capitalized, more transparent, and more willing to lend to productive enterprises rather than rolling over bad loans. Most economists now credit the Okada banking reforms with finally breaking the deflationary feedback loop that had paralyzed the economy since the early 1990s, even though the full benefits would not become apparent until the mid-2000s.

=== Education reform (2000) ===
[[File:Okada Education Reform 2000.png|thumb|right|200px|The National Education Reform Act of 2000 overhauled the Sanese education system]]
The second major initiative of Okada's premiership was the '''National Education Reform Act''' of 2000, a comprehensive overhaul of the Sanese education system that remains one of the most controversial pieces of social legislation of the post-war era.

The reform was driven by Okada's conviction that the education system's emphasis on rote memorization and standardized testing was producing a workforce poorly suited to the demands of a knowledge economy. The Act introduced several major changes:
* Reduced the number of mandatory subjects in secondary schools and introduced elective `track'' programs
* Replaced the university entrance examination system with a comprehensive assessment model that weighted extracurricular activities, personal statements, and teacher recommendations alongside test scores
* Established a '''Curriculum Innovation Fund''' to support schools that developed creative teaching methods
* Mandated a reduction in class sizes to a maximum of 30 students
* Introduced mandatory instruction in `critical thinking and creative problem-solving'' at all levels

The reform was fiercely opposed by conservative educators, parent groups, and LDP traditionalists who argued that it would lower academic standards and undermine the meritocratic foundations of the education system. The controversy was particularly intense because the reform coincided with a period of economic hardship, and many parents feared that any change to the system would disadvantage their children in an already competitive job market.

The long-term effects of the education reform remain debated. Supporters point to improvements in student creativity and problem-solving skills, as measured by international assessments, and to the growth of the technology sector in the 2000s. Critics argue that the reform contributed to a decline in mathematical and scientific literacy and that the comprehensive assessment model for university admissions created opportunities for favoritism and corruption.

=== Coalition politics and the second term (2001-2003) ===
The 2001 general election produced a reduced majority for the LDP, and Okada was forced to reshuffle his coalition. The NTD, which had lost seats in the election, withdrew from the government. Okada replaced it with the [[Okaiken Shintō|Okaiken New Party]] (ONP), forming the LDP-ONP coalition that would govern for the remainder of his premiership.

The coalition with the ONP was more ideologically coherent than the previous arrangement-both parties were center-right and broadly supportive of economic reform-but it was also more fragile. The ONP's leader, [[Yoshito Sengoku]], demanded significant policy concessions in exchange for joining the coalition, including a slowdown in the banking restructuring and increased public works spending for rural constituencies.

Okada's second term was dominated by the ongoing implementation of the banking reforms and the education reform, as well as by growing public dissatisfaction with the pace of economic recovery. Despite the structural improvements in the banking sector, the economy remained sluggish, and unemployment continued to rise. The `Lost Decade'' was now entering its second decade, and public patience was wearing thin.

By late 2002, Okada's approval ratings had fallen below 30%, and LDP factional leaders were openly discussing a leadership change. Okada announced in January 2003 that he would not seek re-election as LDP president, stating: `I was given the task of cleaning out the rot in the banking system. That task is largely complete. The next phase-rebuilding growth-requires a different kind of leadership.'' He was succeeded by [[Yoshito Sengoku]] on 8 March 2003.

=== Foreign policy ===
The Okada government's foreign policy was shaped by the economic crisis and the need to maintain Okaiken's international standing during a period of domestic weakness. The government continued Okaiken's active participation in the [[Adonian Community]], supporting the organization's efforts to deepen economic integration and coordinate monetary policy among member states. Okada personally attended the Adonian Community Economic Summit of 1999, where he advocated for a coordinated response to the global financial instability that had followed the Asian economic crisis.

The government also pursued a significant diplomatic initiative with [[Shansi]]. The Shansian-Sanese Basic Relations Treaty, which had been signed under the Takanaka government, had left the territorial disputes over the northern islands unresolved. Okada authorized the resumption of bilateral negotiations, and the two governments agreed to establish the Joint Northern Territories Commission in 1999, which was tasked with developing a framework for the eventual resolution of the dispute. The Commission's work was slow, but it represented the most significant progress on the territorial issue in decades.

In the Shendan, the Okada government sought to strengthen Okaiken's economic ties through the Shendan Economic Partnership Initiative, which had been launched under the Tokuyama government. The government negotiated bilateral investment agreements with several Shendan nations, and Okada personally led a trade mission to Zong in 2000, where he signed the Zong-Sanese Economic Cooperation Agreement, which expanded the commercial relationship that had been established under the Zong-Sanese Friendship Treaty of 1954.

The government also confronted the growing challenge of Shendan maritime security. The disruption of shipping lanes by piracy and territorial disputes in the southern Shendan threatened Okaiken's commercial interests, and the government authorized the deployment of Sanese naval vessels to participate in the Adonian Community's anti-piracy patrol in the region. The deployment was controversial, as it represented the first overseas operation by the Sanese Self-Defense Forces since their establishment, but it was widely supported as a necessary measure to protect Okaiken's economic interests.

The Okada government also sought to strengthen Okaiken's relationship with Echia, which had been Okaiken's most important diplomatic partner before the war. The Echian-Sanese Cultural Exchange Agreement was renewed in 1999, and the government negotiated the Echian-Sanese Scientific Cooperation Agreement of 2001, which provided for joint research projects in technology, medicine, and environmental science. The agreement was a significant step toward the restoration of the Echian-Sanese relationship, which had been severed during the war and only partially restored in the post-war period.

== Legacy ==
Okada's premiership is remembered primarily for the banking reforms, which are widely regarded as the most consequential economic policy intervention of the [[Lost Decade|Lost Decades]]. While the reforms were deeply unpopular at the time-critics labeled Okada the `undertaker prime minister'' for the number of banks he closed-most economists now agree that they were necessary to break the deflationary spiral and restore the financial system to health.

The education reform of 2000 remains more controversial. While it has been partially retained by subsequent governments, several of its provisions-including the comprehensive assessment model for university admissions-were rolled back under later administrations. The debate between rote memorization and creative education continues to shape Sanese education policy.

Okada's coalition management was competent but uninspiring. His ability to maintain governing coalitions across two different party configurations demonstrated his political skill, but his reliance on the ONP in his second term tied his government to a party that would later be consumed by the corruption scandals of the Sengoku era.

Okada retired from active politics in 2009, having served 33 years in the Diet. He has occasionally written opinion pieces for the ''[[Okami Shimbun]]'' on financial regulation and education policy, and his 2011 memoir, ''{{nihongo|The Reckoning|清算|Seisan}}'', was a bestseller that provided a detailed account of the banking crisis and his government's response.

